L000141	\title{NNLO QCD corrections to hadron production in DIS at finite transverse momentum}
L000142	
L000143	\begin{abstract}
L000144	
L000145	We present the \textit{first} complete calculation of hadron production in deep-inelastic scattering (DIS) at finite transverse momentum to next-to-next-to-leading order (NNLO) in perturbative QCD.
L000146	
L000147	To overcome the long-standing challenge of infrared divergences in semi-inclusive processes with identified final state hadrons at finite transverse momentum, we implement the recently developed $q_T$-subtraction framework based on the recoil-free jet definition.
L000148	
L000149	By utilizing the winner-take-all recombination scheme, we achieve a consistent factorization for hadron-jet associated production, enabling the inclusion of $\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s^3)$ corrections.
L000150	
L000151	Our results demonstrate a significantly improved stabilization of the perturbative expansion and a reduction in scale uncertainties compared to previous next-to-leading order predictions.
L000152	
L000153	We find that the NNLO corrections are essential for a robust description of high precision multiplicity data from the ZEUS collaborations.
L000154	
L000155	This work provides a high precision theoretical foundation for the upcoming Electron-Ion Collider era and establishes a new benchmark for the exploration of the nucleon's three-dimensional structure.
L000156	
L000157	\end{abstract}
L000158	
L000159	
L000160	
L000161	\maketitle
L000162	
L000163	\paragraph*{Introduction.---}
L000164	
L000165	The semi-inclusive deep inelastic scattering (SIDIS) process, $l(l) + N(P) \to l'(l') + h(P_h) + X$, is fundamental to mapping the three-dimensional nucleon structure.
L000166	
L000167	While inclusive DIS has historically provided a precise mapping of collinear parton distribution functions (PDFs), the detection of a final state hadron with finite transverse momentum $P_{hT}$ enables the exploration of the transverse momentum dependent (TMD) dynamics of partons and the hadronization process \cite{Collins:1981uk, Collins:1981uw}.
L000168	
L000169	In particular, the high-$P_{hT}$ region is of great theoretical interest.
L000170	
L000171	It provides a unique window into the transition between the non-perturbative TMD framework and the perturbative collinear factorization regime, where the $P_{hT}$ is generated by hard radiation.
L000172	
L000173	
L000174	
L000175	Over the past decades, experimental programs have provided a detailed mapping of $P_{hT}$ distributions across diverse kinematic ranges.
L000176	
L000177	Early measurements by the H1 \cite{H1:1996muf, H1:1999iry, H1:2004xgw} and ZEUS \cite{ZEUS:1995acw, ZEUS:1999ies} collaborations at HERA established the initial benchmarks for inclusive hadron production at high $P_{hT}$.
L000178	
L000179	These were followed by higher-precision studies from HERMES \cite{HERMES:2012uyd}, COMPASS \cite{COMPASS:2013bfs, COMPASS:2017mvk}, and Jefferson Lab (JLab) \cite{JeffersonLabHallA:2016ctn}, which revealed intricate flavor dependencies and spin-momentum correlations within the nucleon.
L000180	
L000181	
L000182	
L000183	Despite these experimental advances, theoretical predictions for the transverse momentum spectrum have largely been restricted to next-to-leading order (NLO) accuracy \cite{Kniehl:2004hf, Daleo:2004pn, Wang:2019bvb}, which exhibit significant scale uncertainties and struggle to provide a simultaneous global description of experimental data \cite{Gonzalez-Hernandez:2018ipj}.
L000184	
L000185	This status contrasts sharply with rapid progress for inclusive quantities in DIS: N$^3$LO corrections have been computed for single jet production in DIS \cite{Currie:2018fgr}, as well as NNLO corrections for dijet production in DIS \cite{Currie:2017tpe}.
L000186	
L000187	Furthermore, NNLO coefficient functions were recently derived for inclusive SIDIS cross sections~\cite{Abele:2021nyo, Goyal:2023zdi, Bonino:2024qbh, Bonino:2024wgg, Goyal:2024tmo, Goyal:2024emo, Bonino:2025tnf, Haug:2025ava, Bonino:2025qta, Zhou:2025lqv, Bonino:2025bqa, Goyal:2025qyu, Gao:2026tnd}.
L000188	
L000189	
L000190	
L000191	The fully differential description of identified hadrons at finite $P_{hT}$ remains a critical frontier, and achieving NNLO precision is essential to stabilize the perturbative expansion and provide robust theory inputs for the upcoming Electron-Ion Collider (EIC) \cite{Accardi:2012qut, AbdulKhalek:2021gbh}.
L000192	
L000193	However, extending calculations to this order for final states involving identified particles faces significant hurdles due to the complex cancellation of infrared (IR) divergences \cite{Kinoshita:1962ur, Lee:1964is}.
L000194	
L000195	For the description of inclusive quantities, various strategies have emerged to resolve these singularities, broadly categorized into local subtraction \cite{Gehrmann-DeRidder:2005btv, Czakon:2010td, Currie:2013vh}, projection-to-Born methods \cite{Cacciari:2015jma}, and slicing techniques \cite{Catani:2007vq, Gao:2012ja, Gao:2014eea, Gaunt:2015pea, Boughezal:2015dva, Berger:2016inr}.
L000196	
L000197	Yet, applying these techniques to semi-inclusive processes has been limited by the difficulty of consistently treating soft and collinear radiation in the presence of identified hadrons and jets, with a few successful applications~\cite{Fu:2024fgj, Czakon:2025yti, Generet:2025bqx, Gao:2026tnd, Bonino:2026dvr}.
L000198	
L000199	
L000200	In this Letter, we present the \textit{first} complete NNLO QCD calculation of identified hadron production in DIS at finite $P_{hT}$.
L000201	
L000202	The core of our theoretical approach is the implementation of a new $q_T$-subtraction framework recently proposed in Ref.~\cite{Fu:2024fgj, Fu:2026nkd} (see also Refs.~\cite{Buonocore:2022mle, Buonocore:2023rdw, Buonocore:2025ucn}), which generalizes the classical transverse momentum subtraction method~\cite{Catani:2007vq} to final states involving both jets and hadrons.
L000203	
L000204	By utilizing a recoil-free jet axis, specifically the winner-take-all (WTA) recombination scheme~\cite{Salam:WTAUnpublished, Bertolini:2013iqa}, this method allows for the consistent treatment of radiation from the identified hadron and other jets.
L000205	
L000206	By incorporating $\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s^3)$ corrections through this framework, we demonstrate good convergence of theoretical predictions and reduced theoretical uncertainties.
L000207	
L000208	This result marks a new era of precision for SIDIS phenomenology and provides a rigorous test of the QCD factorization theorems in the high-$P_{hT}$ domain.
L000209	
L000210	
L000211	\begin{figure}[t!]
L000212	    \centering
L000213	    \includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{DIS_2jet.pdf}
L000214	    \caption{Schematic definition of the kinematic variables used for the $q_T$-subtraction method in the Breit frame. The incoming virtual photon $\gamma^*$ scatters off the proton, producing an identified hadron and a leading recoiling jet ${\cal J}_k$. The blue lines along the proton direction denote beam collinear radiation. The green curved lines indicate the soft radiation.  The slicing variable is defined by the azimuthal decorrelation $\delta\phi$ (or equivalently the out-of-plane momentum $p_{\rm out}$ of the hadron with respect to the beam-jet plane). The limit $\delta\phi \to 0$ corresponds to the unresolved back-to-back configuration governed by the factorization formula in Eq.~(\ref{eq:fact_phi_jet}).}
L000215	\label{fig:kinematics}
L000216	\end{figure}
L000217	
L000218	\
L000219	\paragraph*{$q_T$-subtraction formalism.---}
L000220	
L000221	In this work, we employ the azimuthal decorrelation $\delta\phi$, or equivalently the hadron momentum component $p_{\rm out}$ perpendicular to the scattering plane, as the slicing variable.
L000222	
L000223	The scattering kinematics in the Breit frame are illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:kinematics} with the scattering plane defined by the leading recoiling jet and beam directions.
L000224	
L000225	This partitions the cross section into contributions from an unresolved region, dominated by soft and collinear radiation, and a resolved region characterized by large-angle emissions.
L000226	
L000227	Explicitly, we have
L000228	\begin{align}
L000229	 {\d\sigma\over \d\mathcal O}= \!\!\int_0^{\delta \phi^{\rm cut}}\d \delta\phi \frac{\d\sigma}{\d\delta \phi\,\d\mathcal O} +\!\!\int_{\delta \phi^{\rm cut}}^{\delta \phi^{\rm max}} \d \delta\phi \frac{\d\sigma}{ \d\delta \phi\,\d\mathcal O},
L000230	\end{align}
L000231	
L000232	where $\delta \phi^{\rm cut}$ is a small resolution/slicing parameter introduced in the $q_T$-subtraction method.
L000233	
L000234	The first (unresolved) part is approximated by the leading-power (LP) contribution in the unresolved limit ($\delta\phi^{\rm cut}\to0$), subject to power corrections which vanish in this limit.
L000235	
L000236	The cancellation of the dependence on $\delta\phi^{\rm cut}$ in the full results serves as a rigorous check of IR safety and numerical stability of our framework.
L000237	
L000238	
L000239	
L000240	
L000241	In the resolved region ($\delta\phi > \delta \phi^{\rm cut}$) where the hadron and jets are well separated, the differential cross sections can be calculated by requiring production of an inclusive hadron in association with a dijet in DIS up to NLO.
L000242	
L000243	We use the one-loop matrix elements for $ei \to ejkl$ and the tree-level matrix elements for $ei \to ejklm$ calculated in Refs.~\cite{Bern:1997sc, Campbell:2002tg, Campbell:2003hd, Campbell:2010ff}, where $ijklm$ represent all possible flavor combinations of QCD partons.
L000244	
L000245	The jets are reconstructed using the $k_T$ algorithm~\cite{Catani:1993hr, Ellis:1993tq} with WTA recombination scheme.
L000246	
L000247	Crucially, the jet algorithm dependence cancels between the resolved and unresolved regions, ensuring the final result is algorithm independent.
L000248	
L000249	The calculations are carried out in the {\tt FMNLO} framework~\cite{Liu:2023fsq} for identified hadron production, with IR singularities handled using a method of dipole subtraction~\cite{Catani:1996vz} in combination with phase space slicing.
L000250	
L000251	
L000252	\begin{figure*}[t!]
L000253	    \centering
L000254	    \includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{afig1.pdf}
L000255	    \caption{
L000256	    Numerical stability of the total cross section with respect to the slicing parameter $\delta\phi^{\rm cut}$.
L000257	
L000258	    The panels display the $\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s^2)$ (red) and $\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s^3)$ (blue) contributions for six representative partonic channels.
L000259	
L000260	    Error bars indicate Monte Carlo statistical uncertainties.
L000261	
L000262	    The solid cyan lines represent the fit to the asymptotic limit $\delta\phi^{\rm cut} \to 0$.
L000263	
L000264	    The dashed purple lines denote the reference values of $\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s^2)$.}
L000265	    \label{fig:cutoff_dep}
L000266	\end{figure*}
L000267	
L000268	
L000269	In the unresolved region ($\delta\phi < \delta \phi^{\rm cut}$), the LP cross section is governed by a factorization formula in impact parameter ($b$) space, which captures the azimuthal decorrelation between the identified hadron and the recoiling jet in the back-to-back limit.
L000270	
L000271	A central feature of our framework is the WTA recombination scheme.
L000272	
L000273	Because the WTA axis is recoil-free, it remains insensitive to soft radiation, providing a stable reference for the hard scattering direction.
L000274	
L000275	This property eliminates non-global logarithms \cite{Dasgupta:2001sh, Neill:2016vbi}, substantially simplifying the structure of the factorization formula \cite{Gutierrez-Reyes:2018qez, Chien:2020hzh}.
L000276	
L000277	Building upon the soft-collinear effective theory (SCET) framework~\cite{Bauer:2000yr, Bauer:2001ct, Bauer:2001yt, Bauer:2002nz, Beneke:2002ph} and following recent developments in Refs.~\cite{Chien:2020hzh, Chien:2022wiq, Fang:2023thw, Fang:2024auf, Fu:2024fgj}, we obtain the factorized cross section
L000278	  \begin{align} \label{eq:fact_phi_jet}
L000279	      &\frac{\d\sigma_{\text{LP}}}{\d x \, \d y \, \d z \, \d^2 \vec P_{hT}  \, \d p_{\rm out}} = \int \frac{\d b}{2\pi}e^{i p_{\rm out} b/\zeta} \\
L000280	  &\hspace{1cm}\times   \sum_{ijk} \int \d \xi\,   H_{ei\to ejk}(Q,\xi,\zeta)\nn\\
L000281	  &\hspace{1cm}\times  {\cal B}_{i/p}(\xi,b)\,
L000282	      {\cal D}_{h/j}(\zeta,b)\,{\cal J}_{k}(b)  \, S_{ij k}(b)\,, \nn
L000283	\end{align}
L000284	where $p_{\rm out}$ is the hadron's transverse momentum relative to the scattering plane.
L000285	
L000286	The standard kinematic variables are defined as
L000287	\begin{align}
L000288	Q^2=-q^2\,,~ x=\frac{Q^2}{2P\cdot q}\,, ~ y=\frac{P\cdot q}{P\cdot l}\,,~z=\frac{P\cdot P_h}{P\cdot q}\,,
L000289	\end{align}
L000290	with virtual photon momentum $q=l-l'$.
L000291	
L000292	The factorized ingredients $H$, $\cal B$, $\cal D$, $S$, and $\mathcal{J}$ represent the hard, TMD beam, fragmentation, soft, and WTA jet functions, respectively, where dependence on the renormalization and rapidity scales $(\mu,\nu)$ is implicit. $\xi$ and $\zeta=z+xP_{hT}^2/[z (\xi-x) Q^2]$ are the momentum fractions for the beam and fragmentation functions.
L000293	
L000294	Finally, the sum $\sum_{ijk}$ runs over all contributing partonic channels, specifically photon-gluon fusion ($\gamma^* g\to q \bar q$) and QCD Compton scattering ($\gamma^* q\to q g$).
L000295	
L000296	
L000297	
L000298	Achieving NNLO accuracy requires two-loop corrections for each factorized ingredient.
L000299	
L000300	The hard function $H_{ei\to ejk}$ encodes the tree-level and virtual corrections to the partonic process $ei \to ejk$.
L000301	
L000302	We extract them from~\cite{Garland:2002ak, Gehrmann:2009vu} and find full agreement with those used in Refs.~\cite{Gehrmann:2022vuk, Gehrmann:2023zpz, NNLOJET:2025rno}.
L000303	
L000304	Crucially, linearly polarized contributions from both incoming and outgoing gluons are essential for a complete description of the cross section beyond LO \cite{Catani:2010pd, Chien:2020hzh,  Gao:2023ivm}.
L000305	
L000306	
L000307	
L000308	We employ standard TMD beam ($\mathcal{B}_{i/p}$) and fragmentation ($\mathcal{D}_{h/j}$) functions~\cite{Collins:2011zzd, Boussarie:2023izj}, applying their well-established NNLO matching onto collinear distribution functions $f_{a/p}$ and $d_{h/b}$ \cite{Catani:2011kr, Catani:2012qa, Gehrmann:2012ze, Gehrmann:2014yya, Luebbert:2016itl, Echevarria:2016scs, Luo:2019hmp, Luo:2019bmw}
L000309	\begin{align}
L000310	  {\cal B}_{i/p}\left(\xi,b\right) &=\sum_a \int_{\xi}^1 \frac{\d {\hat\xi}}{\hat\xi} \, {\cal I}_{i / a}(\hat\xi, b ) \,  f_{a/p}\left(\frac{\xi}{\hat\xi}, \mu\right)\,,   \nonumber \\
L000311	  {\cal D}_{h/j}\left(\zeta,b\right) &=
L000312	\frac{1}{\zeta^2}\sum_b \int_{\zeta}^1 \frac{\d {\hat\zeta }}{\hat\zeta} \, {\cal C}_{b / j}(\hat\zeta, b ) \, d_{h/b}\left(\frac{\zeta}{\hat\zeta}, \mu\right) \,,
L000313	\end{align}
L000314	with ${\cal I}_{i / a}=\delta_{ia}\delta(1-\hat\xi)+\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s)$ and ${\cal C}_{b / j}=\delta_{bj}\delta(1-\hat\zeta)+\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s)$.
L000315	
L000316	The soft function $S_{ijk}$ follows the universal NNLO TMD soft function definition \cite{Echevarria:2015byo, Lubbert:2016rku}, retaining a simple analytic form because the hard scattering process is in a planar configuration  \cite{Gao:2019ojf, Chien:2020hzh, Chien:2022wiq, Gao:2023ivm, Fu:2024fgj}.
L000317	
L000318	
L000319	
L000320	Furthermore, we adopt the $k_T$ algorithm and the WTA recombination scheme for the TMD jet functions $\mathcal{J}_k$, to be consistent with the numerical evaluation in the resolved region.
L000321	
L000322	The NNLO quark jet functions are readily available from recent calculations \cite{Gutierrez-Reyes:2019vbx, Fang:2024auf, Bell:2021dpb, Brune:2022cgr, Buonocore:2025ysd}.
L000323	
L000324	While the NNLO WTA gluon jet functions, comprising unpolarized and linearly polarized components, are currently unknown, renormalization invariance uniquely determines the two-loop logarithmic terms.
L000325	
L000326	We approximate the remaining two constants of the gluon jet functions with rescaled energy-energy correlation jet functions \cite{Luo:2019hmp,Luo:2019bmw}, and find that their numerical impact is negligible.
L000327	
L000328	
L000329	
L000330	\paragraph*{Numerical results.---}
L000331	
L000332	To validate our calculation framework, we explicitly verify that the physical cross section remains independent of the arbitrary cutoff $\delta\phi^{\rm cut}$ in the limit $\delta\phi^{\rm cut}\to 0$.
L000333	
L000334	Fig.~\ref{fig:cutoff_dep} displays the numerical stability of the $\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s^3)$ (NNLO) and $\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s^2)$ (NLO) corrections to the differential cross section of charged pion production at the EIC.
L000335	
L000336	The kinematics are fixed at $\sqrt{s}=141$ GeV, $x=0.2$, $y=0.3$, with integration over $0.2<z<0.8$ and $P_{hT} > z Q/10$.
L000337	
L000338	The results are shown for six representative partonic channels, including $uu$, $ug$, $gu$, $gg$, $u\bar u$, and $u d$, labeled according to the flavors of the incoming and fragmenting partons.
L000339	
L000340	Among these, the $uu$ channel is always dominant due to both favored PDFs and FFs.
L000341	
L000342	We use the CT18 NNLO PDFs~\cite{Hou:2019efy} and the NPC23 NNLO FFs~\cite{Gao:2025hlm} with a nominal choice of $\mu=Q$ for both the renormalization and factorization scales.
L000343	
L000344	The NLO results (red points) exhibit excellent flatness over the range $10^{-3} < \delta\phi^{\rm cut} < 10^{-1}$, confirming a precise cancellation of IR divergences, and converge to the reference values calculated with {\tt FMNLO}~\cite{Liu:2023fsq}.
L000345	
L000346	At NNLO (blue points), the results similarly converge to a constant plateau, despite the larger Monte Carlo integration uncertainties from the resolved cross section.
L000347	
L000348	The solid lines represent a fit to the genuine NNLO corrections together with residual power corrections~\cite{Grazzini:2017mhc, Ebert:2019zkb}.
